// Item prototypes (work item 0010): every item and the blocks that drop
// themselves, in one table; the stack and price classes and the phase
// budgets behind them are in doc/content.md. Loaded and resolved to a
// dense Item_Id at startup, like the blocks.
// name_key is a key into data/strings/en.sjson.
// description_key (optional, work item 0070) is the key of one to three
// sentences the recipe browser shows under a recipe's facts for its first
// output item: describe_item_<id> by convention. A set key must exist.
// category is raw, intermediate, tool, machine or block; the inventory
// sorts by category in that order, then by name.
// stack_size follows doc/content.md: nothing stacks below 50; ore, plates,
// spoils, machines and tools 50, intermediates 100, and the machines placed
// by the dozen (inserters, belts, poles, lamps) 100. Blocks stack like raw
// material.
// places_block (optional) is the block the item places, and mining that
// block yields the item. mined_from (optional) lists further blocks whose
// mining yields the item (grass gives dirt, ore blocks give the ore).
// Every block that can be mined must yield exactly one item.
// fuel_megajoules (optional) is the energy a machine extracts from the item.
// Fluids (water, steam) are not items: fluids do not stack.
// Ore grades (work item 0026): every ore except coal has a low grade twin
// (id ending in _low_grade) that veins yield more of as they deplete. Low
// grade ore does not smelt, except through the low grade iron plate
// schematic: the crusher turns it into crushed ore and the washer crushed
// ore into ore. Coal stays single grade, since it is burned rather than
// refined.
// Byproducts (work item 0027): slag from smelting stacks to 100, gravel
// and mud as before; each has a use (gravel, concrete, clay, brick).
// Slag also places as a slag heap block (work item 0028), the early sink
// before the crusher exists; mining a heap gives the slag back. Mud places
// as the wetland's mud block and mining that gives the mud back (work item
// 0058). The other climate biome blocks give dirt (frozen dirt, dry grass)
// or stone (red and pale rock); snow drops itself.
// The tree species' logs give the log item and their leaves the leaves
// item (work item 0059), so recipes see one kind of wood. Saplings fall
// from decaying leaves after a felling and have no use yet.
// cannot_recycle (optional) keeps the recycler from taking the item even
// when a recipe makes it: raw materials, ore, spoils, slag, mud, crushed
// ore, bricks and concrete.
// Chemistry (work item 0031): plastic bar, sulfur and bitumen from the
// chemical plant, asphalt paving from bitumen and gravel, and science
// pack 2, which needs plastic. The chemical products do not recycle.
// Deep veins (work item 0035): bauxite (with a low grade twin and its
// crushed form), gold ore and quartz come only from deep veins through
// bore drills, so they have no ore blocks. Bauxite washes into alumina,
// the electrolyser turns alumina into aluminium plate and quartz into
// silicon, and the furnace smelts gold ore into gold plate.
// also_mined_from (optional, work item 0036) lists blocks that yield the
// item besides their own drop: gold quartz in cave walls gives quartz
// (mined_from) and gold ore (also_mined_from).
// usable (optional, work item 0036) marks an item the Use_Item action
// reads: the schematics found in cave crates, one per schematic channel
// recipe (recipes.sjson). A usable item places nothing.
// use (optional, work item 0038, default read) is what Use_Item does with
// a usable item: read (a schematic, consumed), assay (the geologist's
// hammer on an outcrop, kept), magnetometer (records the reading, kept;
// detects is the ore item it finds, use_range the blocks it reaches) or
// seismic_shot (a thumper charge fired at the targeted ground, consumed;
// use_range is the radius it images).
// Rocket program (work item 0040): the launch pad, and the rocket parts
// it assembles a rocket from (rocket fuel is a fluid). A part stacks to
// 50, which also caps what one pad slot holds; a rocket takes at most 10.
// tool_tier (optional, tool items only, work item 0051) is the highest
// blocks.sjson tool_tier the item mines by hand while anywhere in the
// player's inventory. Every block tier needs a tool that reaches it.
// price (required, positive, work item 0041) is the venture credit one
// item fetches when a shipment sells it as free trade. The values are
// guesses from the recipe depth: raw material and ore 1, plates 2, steel
// 12, a crafted item about the sum of its inputs, the rare phase 7
// materials (aluminium, silicon, gold) well above their inputs.
// configurable (optional, work item 0202) names what the inventory view's
// configure pop-up sets for every item of the kind: the one value is
// foundation_block (the foundation's block size and height, whose choices
// are in game.sjson). The hint row offers Configure for such an item.
// Any other value is an error.
